Yes. The deeper move is to treat lambda calculus + hardware engineering as a pattern for building other kinds of programmable reality.

Lambda calculus gives you a tiny set of primitives—variables, abstraction, application—from which surprisingly complex computation can be composed. Hardware engineering then gives you the other half: levels of compilation. Boolean algebra → gates → circuits → processors → instruction sets → software. Each layer hides implementation detail while preserving interfaces and invariants.

You can build that same stack for a gauge, a story, a game mechanic, an organization, an emotion, a negotiation, even a social ritual.

For example, a Narrative Calculus might start with objects such as:

A=\text{agent},\quad G=\text{goal},\quad O=\text{obstacle},\quad K=\text{knowledge},\quad R=\text{relationship},\quad S=\text{world state}

Then define operators rather than fixed plots:

Reveal(x)

changes what an agent knows.

Bind(A,G)

attaches a goal to an agent.

Block(G,O)

puts resistance between the agent and the goal.

Transform(A,e)

changes the agent after event e.

A simple story could therefore be represented as something like:

Transform(A,\ Reveal(K,\ Overcome(Block(G,O))))

That isn’t the story. It is closer to the circuit diagram of the story.

Romeo and Juliet, Star Wars, Pokémon, a sales pitch, a political campaign, and a five-minute joke might instantiate radically different surfaces while using some of the same underlying operators.

Then lambda-style abstraction becomes particularly useful.

Instead of:

Darth Vader reveals that he is Luke’s father.

you extract:

RevealHiddenRelationship(parent, protagonist)

Now it is a reusable narrative function:

\lambda x.\ RevealHiddenRelationship(x, protagonist)

Feed it different worlds:

f(\text{space opera})

f(\text{mafia drama})

f(\text{Pokémon rival story})

The costume changes. The operator survives.

That’s almost exactly what you were describing with transplanting gameplay mechanics between games.

