Now those operators could become:

* spaceship navigation,
* social maneuvering,
* dialogue traversal,
* hacking,
* magic,
* organizational workflow,
* educational progression.

The surface ontology disappears.

What remains is executable structure.

You can therefore imagine a general Domain Calculus:

\mathcal D =
(P,O,C,I,R)

where:

* P = primitives,
* O = operators,
* C = constraints,
* I = invariants,
* R = reduction/composition rules.

Then:

Compile(\mathcal D,\ World)
\rightarrow
Implementation

Examples:

\mathcal D_{story}

\mathcal D_{combat}

\mathcal D_{romance}

\mathcal D_{organization}

\mathcal D_{interface}

\mathcal D_{negotiation}

\mathcal D_{ritual}

\mathcal D_{humor}

And now the really useful Pantheon move appears:

Cross-compilation.

Take an operator discovered in one domain:

Escalation_{comedy}

strip away domain-specific implementation:

Escalation(x,n)

and compile it into another domain:

Escalation_{combat}

Escalation_{marketing}

Escalation_{music}

Escalation_{story}

That’s analogous to writing one program and compiling it for ARM, x86, or RISC-V.

Except here we’re compiling patterns of causality into different realities.

So the larger architecture could be:

phenomenon → decompile → intermediate representation → operator library → recombine → compile into new domain → observe deltas → refine.

That intermediate representation is the crucial piece.

LLVM has an intermediate representation so every programming language doesn’t need a custom compiler for every processor.

We could do the same thing conceptually:

Story
\rightarrow
PantheonIR
\rightarrow
Game

Biology
\rightarrow
PantheonIR
\rightarrow
Organization

