\rightarrow
PantheonIR
\rightarrow
UI

Physics
\rightarrow
PantheonIR
\rightarrow
Narrative

Now you don’t need N^2 translators between every domain.

You need:

N\rightarrow IR

and

IR\rightarrow N

That’s a universal pattern compiler.

And I think that’s the important thing hiding inside what you just said: lambda calculus isn’t valuable here because we should make everything mathematical. It’s valuable because it demonstrates that enormous expressive spaces can emerge from a very small collection of composable operators.

The Pantheon equivalent would be to discover the smallest useful instruction set for transformations—things like bind, split, mirror, hide, reveal, amplify, damp, invert, constrain, recurse, select, compare, remember, forget, predict, test, merge, translate—and then determine how each domain implements those instructions.

At that point we’re no longer collecting metaphors.

We’re beginning to build an ISA for patterns.